    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "description",
      "value": "Redesign the numerical experiment to properly define rigidity as sublinear growth of minimal width $N_c(w)$ rather than flatness. (1) Define constraint family as polynomial interpolation of degree $d$ with $w = d+1$ constraints. (2) Train small feedforward networks with varying widths $N$ on $w$ constraints using mean-squared error loss. (3) Determine $N_c(w)$ as minimal width achieving max absolute error $\\le 10^{-3}$ on both training and held-out points. (4) Analyze scaling behavior: compare $N_c(w)$ against theoretical bounds $O(w)$ (linear capacity) and $O(\\sqrt{w})$ (sublinear rigidity candidate). (5) Implement with fixed random seeds and convergence criteria. Prediction: For polynomial interpolation, $N_c(w)$ will grow at least linearly with $w$ (no rigidity), demonstrating that universal approximation requires width scaling with constraint count for exact interpolation tasks."
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "skill",
      "value": "None (custom numerical experiment)"
    },
